\title{Installing Julia and CoTiMA2:\\
  A Copy-and-Paste Guide for Windows, macOS, and Ubuntu/Linux}
\author{CoTiMA2 development documentation}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

<<setup, include=FALSE, purl=FALSE>>=
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  echo = TRUE, eval = FALSE, purl = FALSE,
  warning = FALSE, message = FALSE
)
@

\begin{abstract}
This guide is intended for colleagues who want to install a shared CoTiMA2
source-package file and use its optional Julia estimation backend. No prior
experience with Julia or command-line tools is assumed. Separate instructions
are provided for Windows, macOS, and Ubuntu/Linux. Every command is labelled
according to the program in which it must be entered. The guide also explains
the common situation in which updating R creates a new, initially empty R
package library.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{What will be installed?}

The installation has four distinct parts:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{R} and preferably RStudio, which you may already have.
  \item \textbf{CoTiMA2}, supplied as a file whose name ends in
    \code{.tar.gz}, for example \code{CoTiMA2\_0.14.5.tar.gz}.
  \item \textbf{Julia}, a separate programming language used by the optional
    Julia estimation backend.
  \item \textbf{JuliaCall}, an R package that allows R and Julia to
    communicate.
\end{enumerate}

CoTiMA2 includes its own Julia project description. The command
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup()} installs and precompiles the Julia packages required by
that project. Colleagues do not need to learn Julia or manually select Julia
packages.

\important{Installing CoTiMA2 and installing Julia are separate operations.
The CoTiMA2 tarball does not contain Julia itself. An active internet
connection is required when Julia and the Julia packages are installed for the
first time.}

\section{Recognizing the different command windows}

Installation problems often arise because a valid command is pasted into the
wrong program. This guide uses the following three labels.

\begin{longtable}{>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.19\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.34\linewidth}
                  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.37\linewidth}}
\toprule
Label & How to open it & What it looks like \\
\midrule
\endhead
System terminal & Windows Terminal, Command Prompt, PowerShell, macOS
Terminal, or Ubuntu Terminal & A separate text window outside RStudio. Its
prompt may end in \code{\$}, \code{\%}, or \code{>}. \\
R console & The Console pane in RStudio, or the R application & It normally
shows the prompt \code{>}. R commands such as \code{install.packages()} belong
here. \\
Julia prompt & A Julia window started with the command \code{julia} & It shows
the prompt \code{julia>}. None of the installation commands in this guide need
to be pasted there unless explicitly stated. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

When copying a displayed command, copy only the text inside the grey box. Do
not copy prompt characters such as \code{>}, \code{\$}, or \code{julia>}.

\section{Step 1: install Julia}

The Julia project recommends the Juliaup installer and version manager. It
installs a current stable Julia version, makes updates easier, and normally
configures the command search path automatically.

\subsection{Windows}

\subsubsection*{Recommended graphical method}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Open the \menu{Microsoft Store} from the Windows Start menu.
  \item Search for \menu{Julia}.
  \item Select the official Julia application and click \menu{Install}.
  \item When installation has finished, close and reopen RStudio if it was
    already running.
\end{enumerate}

Alternatively, use the official command-line installation:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Open the Start menu.
  \item Type \menu{Windows Terminal} and open it. If Windows Terminal is not
    available, open \menu{PowerShell} or \menu{Command Prompt}.
  \item Paste the command below and press Enter.
\end{enumerate}

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
winget install --name Julia --id 9NJNWW8PVKMN -e -s msstore
\end{lstlisting}

Close the terminal after installation, open a new terminal window, paste the
following command, and press Enter:

\begin{lstlisting}
julia --version
\end{lstlisting}

A successful result begins with \code{julia version}. The exact version number
may be newer than the one shown in screenshots or examples.

\subsection{macOS}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Press Command--Space to open Spotlight.
  \item Type \menu{Terminal} and press Return.
  \item Paste the following command into Terminal and press Return.
\end{enumerate}

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
curl -fsSL https://install.julialang.org | sh
\end{lstlisting}

The installer may ask for confirmation. Accept the default installation by
following the instruction printed in the terminal. When it has finished,
close Terminal completely and open a new Terminal window. Then verify the
installation:

\begin{lstlisting}
julia --version
\end{lstlisting}

If macOS or an institutional security tool asks whether Terminal may access
downloaded files or the network, allow the operation. A graphical Julia
installer is also available from \url{https://julialang.org/downloads/}, but
Juliaup is preferred because it also manages updates and the command path.

\subsection{Ubuntu and other Linux systems}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Press Ctrl--Alt--T to open the Ubuntu Terminal. On another Linux
    desktop, open the application named \menu{Terminal}.
  \item Paste the following command and press Enter.
\end{enumerate}

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
curl -fsSL https://install.julialang.org | sh
\end{lstlisting}

Accept the default installation when prompted. Do not add \code{sudo} to this
command. Close the terminal completely, open a new terminal, and verify Julia:

\begin{lstlisting}
julia --version
\end{lstlisting}

\important{Do not use \code{sudo apt install julia} for this setup. The Julia
project recommends Juliaup or official Julia binaries because Julia packages
provided by Linux distributions can be outdated or built with incompatible
dependencies.}

If the first command reports that \code{curl} is missing, install only
\code{curl} using these two terminal commands, and then repeat the Juliaup
command:

\begin{lstlisting}
sudo apt update
sudo apt install curl
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Common Julia verification commands}

These commands are optional but useful when asking for help. Enter them in a
system terminal, one line at a time:

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
julia --version
juliaup status
\end{lstlisting}

On Windows, the following command shows the executable location:

\begin{lstlisting}
where julia
\end{lstlisting}

On macOS or Linux, use:

\begin{lstlisting}
command -v julia
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Step 2: prepare the R package library}

Start RStudio. All commands in this section belong in the R console.

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
R.version.string
.libPaths()
\end{lstlisting}

R normally uses a separate package library for each R release. Consequently,
after an update such as R 4.5 to R 4.6, the new R session may not see packages
that were installed for the older R version. This is normal and does not mean
that the CoTiMA2 tarball is damaged.

The following R code creates the personal package library, makes it active,
and selects the official CRAN cloud mirror:

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
user_library <- Sys.getenv("R_LIBS_USER")
dir.create(user_library, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
.libPaths(c(user_library, .libPaths()))
options(repos = c(CRAN = "https://cloud.r-project.org"))
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Step 3: install the required R packages}

Paste the complete block below into the R console and press Enter. R installs
only packages that are not already available to the current R version.

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
required_packages <- c(
  "OpenMx", "ctsem", "lavaan", "foreach", "Matrix", "MBESS",
  "crayon", "psych", "doParallel", "rootSolve", "abind",
  "RPushbullet", "openxlsx", "zcurve", "scholar", "stringi",
  "parallelly", "JuliaCall", "posterior", "knitr"
)

missing_packages <- required_packages[
  !vapply(required_packages, requireNamespace,
          quietly = TRUE, FUN.VALUE = logical(1))
]

if (length(missing_packages)) {
  install.packages(missing_packages, dependencies = TRUE)
}
\end{lstlisting}

This step can take considerable time because packages such as OpenMx, ctsem,
and their dependencies are large. Messages printed in red are not always
errors. Wait until the R prompt \code{>} returns. Then check again:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
missing_packages <- required_packages[
  !vapply(required_packages, requireNamespace,
          quietly = TRUE, FUN.VALUE = logical(1))
]
missing_packages
\end{lstlisting}

The desired result is \code{character(0)}. If package names are printed, copy
the complete installation error when asking for help.

\subsection{Additional Ubuntu preparation if R packages fail to compile}

Use this only when Ubuntu reports missing compilers or missing system headers.
Close neither R nor RStudio, but open a separate Ubuntu Terminal. Paste the
following commands into the terminal and press Enter after each block:

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
sudo apt update
sudo apt install build-essential gfortran \
  libcurl4-openssl-dev libssl-dev libxml2-dev \
  libgsl-dev libglpk-dev libopenblas-dev
\end{lstlisting}

Enter your Ubuntu password if requested. The password is not displayed while
you type; this is normal. Return to RStudio and repeat the R package
installation block above.

\section{Step 4: install the shared CoTiMA2 tarball}

Save the supplied \code{CoTiMA2\_x.y.z.tar.gz} file somewhere easy to find,
for example in Downloads. Do not unzip it.

The easiest platform-independent method opens a file-selection window:

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
cotima_tarball <- file.choose()
install.packages(cotima_tarball, repos = NULL, type = "source")
\end{lstlisting}

After running the first line, select the shared \code{.tar.gz} file. The second
line installs it. If installation reports that dependencies are unavailable,
repeat Step 3 and then retry this step.

Restart R using \menu{Session $>$ Restart R} in RStudio. Then verify the
installation in the R console:

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
library(CoTiMA2)
packageVersion("CoTiMA2")
\end{lstlisting}

The printed version should match the tarball filename.

\section{Step 5: install and precompile the Julia packages for CoTiMA2}

This is normally required only once for a Julia installation. In the R
console, run:

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
library(CoTiMA2)
julia_setup <- ctmaJuliaSetup()
\end{lstlisting}

This command performs several tasks in separate Julia processes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item it locates the Julia executable;
  \item it prepares the Julia packages needed by JuliaCall;
  \item it installs the exact Julia dependencies recorded by CoTiMA2; and
  \item it precompiles those dependencies.
\end{itemize}

The first setup may take several minutes. On slower computers or institutional
networks it can take substantially longer. Leave RStudio open and wait until
the R prompt returns. A successful setup ends with the message
\code{Julia setup completed successfully}.

You can then display what was found:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
julia_setup$julia_version
julia_setup$julia_home
julia_setup$project
\end{lstlisting}

\important{After \code{ctmaJuliaSetup()} has completed, restart R before the
first Julia fit. Also restart R after a failed JuliaCall initialization or
before changing the requested number of Julia threads.}

\section{Step 6: a final installation check}

After restarting R, paste the following into the R console:

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
library(CoTiMA2)

julia_check <- ctmaJuliaSetup(
  bootstrap_juliacall = FALSE,
  instantiate = FALSE,
  precompile = FALSE
)

julia_check$julia_version
julia_check$julia_home
\end{lstlisting}

This check does not reinstall packages or initialize embedded Julia. It only
verifies that CoTiMA2 can find and execute Julia. If it succeeds, the same R
session may be used for the first fit. Once a fit initializes JuliaCall,
however, Julia's thread count is fixed for the remainder of that R session.

For an initial analysis, use a conservative thread count. Four or eight Julia
threads are often a better first test than using every logical CPU:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
fit <- ctmaFit(
  ctmaInitFit = my_initial_fit,
  backend = "julia",
  backendArgs = list(
    julia_threads = 4L,
    blas_threads = 1L
  )
)
\end{lstlisting}

Replace \code{my\_initial\_fit} with the name of the actual CoTiMA initial-fit
object. This final code is an illustration and cannot run until such an object
has been created or loaded.

\section{After updating R}

An R update is the most common reason why an installation that worked
yesterday appears to be broken. Julia may still be installed correctly, while
the new R version uses a new library and cannot find JuliaCall, ctsem, OpenMx,
or other packages.

Use the following recovery sequence in the new R version:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Repeat Step 2 to activate the new personal R library.
  \item Repeat Step 3 to install missing R packages for the new R version.
  \item Reinstall the CoTiMA2 tarball as described in Step 4.
  \item Rebuild Julia's connection to the new R installation with the code
    below.
\end{enumerate}

\rnote
\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
library(CoTiMA2)
ctmaJuliaSetup(
  bootstrap_juliacall = TRUE,
  r_home = R.home()
)
\end{lstlisting}

Restart R after this command. The option
\code{bootstrap\_juliacall = TRUE} forces Julia's RCall package to be rebuilt
against the currently running R installation.

\section{Troubleshooting}

\subsection{R says that dependencies are not available}

The tarball was usually built successfully; the current R library is missing
its dependencies. Run Steps 2 and 3. Do not repeatedly rebuild or unzip the
CoTiMA2 tarball.

\subsection{CoTiMA2 cannot find Julia}

First verify Julia in a system terminal using \code{julia --version}. Close
and reopen RStudio afterward. Juliaup commonly installs launchers at
\code{\textasciitilde/.juliaup/bin/julia} on macOS/Linux, while Windows uses
\code{julia.exe}. CoTiMA2 also searches Juliaup's versioned Linux directories.

If automatic discovery still fails, obtain the path using \code{where julia}
on Windows or \code{command -v julia} on macOS/Linux. Then pass the complete
path in R, for example:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
ctmaJuliaSetup(julia = "/complete/path/to/julia")
\end{lstlisting}

Windows paths may be written with forward slashes:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
ctmaJuliaSetup(
  julia = "C:/Users/your-name/.juliaup/bin/julia.exe"
)
\end{lstlisting}

The exact Windows location can differ; use the path reported by
\code{where julia} rather than copying the example literally.

\subsection{JuliaCall failed once and every retry fails immediately}

Restart R. JuliaCall remains partly initialized after some failures and cannot
always be repaired inside the same R session. After restarting, run
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup()} before attempting another fit.

\subsection{The requested thread count is ignored}

Julia's thread count is fixed when JuliaCall initializes Julia. Restart R and
make the fit the first Julia operation in that R session. Supply the desired
count through \code{backendArgs}, for example
\code{backendArgs = list(julia\_threads = 4L, blas\_threads = 1L)}.

\subsection{Ubuntu reports status 139 or native-library errors}

RStudio Server, Java, or an R installation can place incompatible libraries
in \code{LD\_LIBRARY\_PATH} or \code{LD\_PRELOAD}. CoTiMA2's
\code{ctmaJuliaSetup()} automatically removes those variables from its Linux
setup subprocesses. Run setup through CoTiMA2 rather than manually invoking
Julia package commands from inside RStudio:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
library(CoTiMA2)
ctmaJuliaSetup(bootstrap_juliacall = TRUE)
\end{lstlisting}

Restart R afterward.

\subsection{Downloads fail behind a university or company firewall}

Julia and its package manager require HTTPS access to official Julia
infrastructure, including \code{julialang.org} and its subdomains. Ask local IT
support to allow HTTPS traffic to the official Julia domains. A proxy or
security product that replaces certificates can also prevent package
downloads.

\subsection{What information should be sent when asking for help?}

Run the following in the R console and copy the complete output:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=R]
R.version.string
Sys.info()[c("sysname", "release", "machine")]
.libPaths()
packageVersion("CoTiMA2")
packageVersion("JuliaCall")

check <- ctmaJuliaSetup(
  bootstrap_juliacall = FALSE,
  instantiate = FALSE,
  precompile = FALSE
)
check$julia
check$julia_version
check$julia_home
\end{lstlisting}

Also include the complete error message, beginning several lines before the
word \code{Error}. Screenshots can omit important text; copied console output
is preferable.

\section{Updating Julia later}

Juliaup can update the stable Julia release. Open a system terminal, paste the
following command, and press Enter:

\terminalnote
\begin{lstlisting}
juliaup update
\end{lstlisting}

After a Julia update, restart R and rerun \code{ctmaJuliaSetup()} in the R
console. Do not update Julia in the middle of a running analysis.

